new evacuations are underway in a deadly  and growing wildfire in Northern  California the so called car fire has  burned nearly 150 square miles and  officials say it's only about 17 percent  contained about 40,000 people have been  forced from their homes the fire has  killed six people including three  members of one family 70 year-old melody  Bledsoe and her two grandchildren died  in their home the car fire is one of  nine major wildfires in California  Carter Evans is in Redding north of  Sacramento where he spoke with Bledsoe's  husband Carter good morning good morning  this man's story is absolutely  heartbreaking he told me how he only  left his home not far from here on  Thursday for a few minutes to run an  errand it was about 15 minutes later  when he got a frantic call from his wife  saying the fire was at their backdoor I  was only going about 15 minutes when my  wife called me and said you got to get  here the fire is coming up the hill Edie  Bledsoe says he had no idea his home was  in danger when he left his wife melody  with their two great grandchildren ages  four and five I was talking to my little  grandson on the phone he's saying  grandpa please you got to come in  Hilversum fires at the back door I said  I said I'm right by honey just hold on  grandpa's coming but he says the road  was blocked with cars and the flames  stopped him from returning on foot other  died right there with them they're that  important to me  fire officials near Redding expanded  their evacuation orders Sunday more than  38,000 people have been forced to flee  their homes since Thursday officials say  the fire is now threatening communities  outside of Redding this time-lapse video  shows the fires intensity creating winds  strong enough to uproot trees at  Bludso's home is one of more than 850  structures that have been destroyed did  you get any sort of evacuation warning  nothing absolutely not a word nobody  told us nothing if I'd had any kind of  warning I'd have never ever left my  family in that house he said he didn't  hear any evacuation warning we're  looking into that Shasta County Sheriff  Tom  Senko says his department did tell  people living in Redding to evacuate  they're investigating to see if the  Bledsoe home got a warning call or a  door knob and the area's both before the  Bludso's home and after the Bledsoe home  there was evidence that notifications  were made for the door-to-door  notifications now authorities say one of  the people who was killed  ignored evacuation orders and  authorities are facing another problem  here as well looting so far three people  have been arrested including one man in  camouflage and carrying a loaded gun  according to police Alex Carter that is  a tough tough story to hear  heartbreaking gut-wrenching the Bludso's  family our prayers go out to them
 

